= Chronotype — Simple =

<onlyinclude>A person's '''chronotype''' describes their tendency to sleep, wake and be active at earlier or later times of day relative to other people. Someone may naturally prefer earlier mornings, while someone else feels more alert later in the evening. Chronotype is related to the body's circadian timing system, but it is not an unchanging personality type and cannot be determined reliably from one night's bedtime alone.<ref name="horne">J. A. Horne and O. Östberg, [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1027738/ “A Self-Assessment Questionnaire to Determine Morningness-Eveningness in Human Circadian Rhythms”], ''International Journal of Chronobiology'' 4(2), 97–110 (1976).</ref>

== Morning and evening preferences ==

A morning-oriented person may find early activities easier and prefer going to sleep relatively early. An evening-oriented person may become sleepy later and prefer waking later.

Most people do not fit neatly into two categories. Preferences and sleep timing vary with age, work schedules, daylight exposure, family responsibilities and health.

A person's regular bedtime may reflect what their job requires rather than their internal preference.

== How is chronotype studied? ==

Researchers use questionnaires about preferred activity and sleep times, sleep diaries and physiological signals related to circadian phase. Horne and Östberg introduced a morningness–eveningness questionnaire, while Roenneberg and colleagues developed a questionnaire-based approach distinguishing sleep on workdays and free days.<ref name="roenneberg">Till Roenneberg, Anna Wirz-Justice and Martha Merrow, [https://doi.org/10.1177/0748730402239679 “Life between Clocks: Daily Temporal Patterns of Human Chronotypes”], ''Journal of Biological Rhythms'' 18, 80–90 (2003).</ref>

No single questionnaire score establishes a complete biological clock measurement. Sleep debt, environmental demands and interpretation of questions matter.

== What is social jetlag? ==

The phrase '''social jetlag''' (introduced in research by Wittmann and colleagues in 2006)<ref name="socialjetlag">M. Wittmann, J. Dinich, M. Merrow and T. Roenneberg, [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16687322/ Social jetlag: misalignment of biological and social time], ''Chronobiology International'' 23, 497–509 (2006).</ref> describes a mismatch between sleep timing on workdays and free days. Imagine someone sleeps from 11 PM to 7 AM on workdays and from 1 AM to 9 AM on free days. The middle of their sleep shifts from 3 AM to 5 AM, a two-hour difference.

This difference can be measured, but it does not prove that the person's underlying circadian clock is two hours later: work constraints and accumulated sleep debt may explain part of the shift.

== Is chronotype fixed? ==

No. Chronotype often changes over a person's lifetime. Population questionnaire data show later sleep timing across adolescence and a subsequent tendency toward earlier timing in adulthood; individual trajectories vary.<ref name="age">T. Roenneberg et al., [https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15620633/ A marker for the end of adolescence], ''Current Biology'' 14, R1038–R1039 (2004).</ref> Light exposure, changing schedules and biological differences can also influence timing.

Chronotype is not a diagnosis. A person with an evening preference does not automatically have a sleep disorder, and a morning preference does not guarantee adequate sleep.

== Why does it matter? ==

Timing preference can help researchers study learning, work schedules, activity and sleep. But conclusions need to consider how long someone sleeps, their health, time-zone and daylight conditions, and how the study defines performance.

Advice such as “everyone should wake at 5 AM” ignores real individual and environmental differences. Conversely, describing every late sleeper as a night owl with a fixed biological identity is also too simple.

== What is not established? ==

Chronotype does not determine intelligence, moral character, job success or a universal optimal daily schedule. Two people with the same questionnaire score may have different circadian timing and different sleep pressures.

